The sequence $x_n$ is in $\ell_1$, meaning $\sum |x_n| < \infty$. For $p > 1$, since $|x_n| \leq 1$ for sufficiently large $n$, we have $|x_n|^p \leq |x_n|$, so $\sum |x_n|^p$ converges by comparison. Thus, $x_n \in \ell_p$. Convergence in $\ell_1$ implies that the difference $x_n - c$ has $\ell_1$-norm tending to 0. For $p > 1$, the $\ell_p$-norm of $x_n - c$ is bounded by the $\ell_1$-norm, as $|x_n - c|^p \leq |x_n - c|$ when $|x_n - c| \leq 1$. Hence, $\|x_n - c\|_p \to 0$, so convergence in $\ell_1$ implies convergence in $\ell_p$.

**Final Answer**
\boxed{Yes}

